## Why This Is Asked
Tests for streaming pipelines are critical at scale; this angle emphasizes end-to-end guarantees, replay safety, and schema evolution beyond static REST tests.

## Key Concepts
- Streaming end-to-end testing with Kafka
- Exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics
- Windowed aggregations correctness
- Schema evolution and compatibility
- Backpressure and fault injection
- Canary deployments and CI validation

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test: idempotent ingestion with duplicate events
function testIdempotentIngestion() {
  const events = generateEvents({n: 1000, duplicates: true});
  ingest(events);
  ingest(events);
  const result = readWindowedAggStore();
  assert(result.latestWindowSum === expectedSum);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you model time in tests for replay vs processing time?
- How would you verify exactly-once semantics with out-of-order events?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests practical ML-in-prod testing: data validation, drift, bias, and safe rollouts in streaming systems.

## Key Concepts
- Canary testing; latency budget; drift detection; fairness metrics
- Data validation; feature store integrity; schema evolution
- CI/CD integration; monitoring dashboards; rollback criteria

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch
function testCanaryLatencyAndDrift(baseline, candidate, stream) {
  const latency = measureLatency(stream, candidate);
  const drift = new AUROCDriftDetector(baseline, candidate).delta();
  if (!(latency < 0.15)) throw new Error('Latency budget failed');
  if (!(drift < 0.01)) throw new Error('AUROC drift too high');
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you handle schema changes across versions?
- How would you evaluate fairness across demographic groups?

## Why This Is Asked

This question probes practical end-to-end testing of a distributed MongoDB search feature, focusing on ranking accuracy, fuzziness robustness, pagination, performance KPIs, and resilience under failover and repartitioning. It also emphasizes realistic CI integration and data generation strategies.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end search tests for ranking and fuzziness
- Performance metrics: p95 latency, throughput, error rate
- Resilience: replica failover, shard migration, partitions
- Data modeling: synthetic boosts and realistic term distributions
- CI data management and flaky-test handling

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example test sketch (pseudo)
async function testSearchRanking() {
  const res = await api.search('coffee', { fuzziness: 1, page: 1, perPage: 10 })
  if (res.results.length !== 10) throw new Error('unexpected length')
  if (res.results[0].score < res.results[1].score) throw new Error('ranking not sorted')
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate ranking under load spikes and index churn?
- How would you detect and isolate flaky tests caused by replication lag?

## Why This Is Asked
Examines end-to-end streaming test strategy, including schema evolution, hot-swapping workers, and fault tolerance under real-world load.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end streaming tests with at-least-once semantics
- Schema evolution compatibility and registry integration
- Canary deployments, fault tolerance, and ordering guarantees
- Idempotent sinks and replayable test data

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo test scaffold for streaming end-to-end
const testEventsV1 = generateEvents(1000, {version:1});
produceToKafka(testEventsV1);
updateSchemaRegistry({version:2});
const results = consumeFromSink();
assertOrdering(results);
assertDuplicates(results) < 0.001;
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you quantify the impact of duplicates on downstream analytics?
- What automated rollback criteria would you implement for canaries?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests for a financial API must cover correctness, idempotency, and resilience to partial failures. This question probes practical plan-building, basic data modeling, and how to verify that auditing is preserved under retries.

## Key Concepts
- Idempotency and clientOrderId handling
- Functional vs integration vs resilience testing
- Audit/logging verification under failure and retry

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-test sketch for idempotent retry
test('idempotent /orders retries return same result', async () => {
  const payload = { userId: 'u1', symbol: 'AAPL', side: 'BUY', quantity: 10, clientOrderId: 'c-123' };
  const first = await post('/orders', payload);
  const second = await post('/orders', payload);
  expect(first.id).toBe(second.id);
  expect(db.orders.filter(o => o.clientOrderId === 'c-123').length).toBe(1);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you extend tests to cover concurrent order submissions with different clientOrderId values?
- What changes would you make if the DB supports atomic upserts but not true transactions?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design end-to-end, fault-tolerant data pipelines and reason about exactly-once vs at-least-once semantics in real-time systems.

## Key Concepts

- End-to-end testing of streaming pipelines
- Idempotency and deduplication strategies
- Replay, backpressure, and regional outage resilience
- Metrics: data loss, duplicates, latency, SLAs

## Code Example

```javascript
// Simple dedup: track seen event_ids to skip duplicates in sinks
const seen = new Set();
function process(event){
  if(seen.has(event.event_id)) return false; // duplicate
  seen.add(event.event_id);
  // write to sink
  return true;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test cross-region failover and data skew in this pipeline?
- What changes would you make if sinks cannot guarantee exactly-once delivery?

## Why This Is Asked
This assesses practical, beginner-friendly end-to-end testing skills focusing on input validation, idempotency, queueing, rate limiting, and privacy in logs; uses realistic dataflow rather than theory.

## Key Concepts
- End-to-end testing with real components vs mocks
- Idempotency keys for duplicate requests
- Rate-limiting and concurrency testing
- Log scrubbing and PII protection
- CI reproducibility with fixtures

## Code Example
```javascript
// Jest + supertest skeleton for /feedback endpoint
const request = require('supertest');
const app = require('../src/app');

test('idempotent submission', async () => {
  const payload = { user: 'alice', feedback: 'great', idempotencyKey: 'abc-123' };
  await request(app).post('/feedback').send(payload).expect(201);
  // second attempt with same key should be treated as duplicate
  await request(app).post('/feedback').send(payload).expect(409);
});
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt tests for non-deterministic services (e.g., occasional DB hiccups)?
- What strategies would you use to measure and reduce test flakiness in CI?

